      "writtenOn": "2026-10-16T03:43:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Should we use `PKVM_PAGE_SHARED_BORROWED` (with `PKVM_ID_HYP`) here instead of `PKVM_PAGE_NONE`?\n\nI understand the commit message notes parity with ARM (where `.hyp.text` uses `PKVM_NOPAGE`), but ARM\u0027s `struct hyp_page` model is different here:\n1. On ARM64, `struct hyp_page` has no `page-\u003eowner` field (it only stores `hyp_state` and `host_state`), and ARM64 does not have `pkvm_host_donate_hyp_share_ro()`. In ARM, `host_state \u003d\u003d PKVM_PAGE_SHARED_BORROWED` is only used for guest pages shared with the host (`__pkvm_guest_share_host()`).\n\n2. On pKVM-x86, `struct pkvm_page` explicitly tracks `page-\u003eowner \u003d PKVM_ID_HYP` alongside `page-\u003ehost_state`, and we already have a dedicated canonical state for a `PKVM_ID_HYP` RAM page mapped Read-Only in `host_mmu`: `(PKVM_PAGE_SHARED_BORROWED,\n  PKVM_ID_HYP)` (used by `pkvm_host_donate_hyp_share_ro()`, State `S5: HYP_SHARED_RO` in go/page-custody-battles), whereas `(PKVM_PAGE_NONE, PKVM_ID_HYP)` (`S4: HYP_EXCLUSIVE`) strictly means unmapped in `host_mmu`.\n\nUsing `(PKVM_PAGE_SHARED_BORROWED, PKVM_ID_HYP)` keeps the `PKVM_PAGE_NONE \u003c\u003d\u003e unmapped in host_mmu` invariant intact and avoids introducing an 8th RAM meta-state on x86.",
      "revId": "651d8890f177e183a9727fdc9abd3607ef45f8ff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
